.01525115966796875, -0.0018644332885742188, 0.027587890625, 0.007762908935546875, -0.01557159423828125, 0.052154541015625, -0.036956787109375, 0.0218658447265625, 0.0187530517578125, 0.006481170654296875, 0.0311431884765625, 0.0142974853515625, 0.0310211181640625, -0.011810302734375, 0.037322998046875, 0.005367279052734375, 0.0294952392578125, -0.0142822265625, 0.00563812255859375, 0.005725860595703125, -0.018646240234375, 0.01605224609375, -0.0175323486328125, 0.00311279296875, -0.0011911392211914062, 0.042816162109375, -0.01216888427734375, -0.0187835693359375, -0.0236663818359375, -0.006931304931640625, -0.0120086669921875, -0.0112152099609375, 0.02764892578125, 0.030242919921875, 0.00128173828125, -0.0284576416015625, -0.0023288726806640625, 0.002796173095703125, 0.0031585693359375, -0.0298309326171875, 0.02935791015625, -0.045379638671875, -0.01171875, -0.0014495849609375, 0.00826263427734375, 0.0272674560546875, -0.00782012939453125, -0.007297515869140625, -0.002735137939453125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Large-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, most existing pipelines optimize datasets for individual tasks in isolation, limiting the ability to leverage shared knowledge across related generative tasks. In this work, we introduce a **capability-driven data infrastructure**\u2014where a \"capability\" refers to a distinct generative skill such as text-to-image grounding, image editing, or associating images with structured knowledge. This framing enables systematic organization and integration of supervision for multiple generative capabilities, supporting curriculum-based training and interoperability across tasks. Unlike prior work, which typically focuses on single-task curation or ad hoc multi-task extensions (e.g., Ramesh et al., 2022; Saharia et al., 2022; Chen et al., 2023), our framework explicitly models dependencies between capabilities (e.g., grounding as a prerequisite for editing), integrates data engines with shared annotation layers, and schedules data exposure according to a principled curriculum. Table 1 (see Appendix) summarizes key differences: prior pipelines either lack explicit modeling of task dependencies or rely on static multi-task datasets, whereas our approach unifies data curation, annotation, and curriculum scheduling across capabilities.\n\n**Motivation and Conceptual Clarity:** The motivation for this approach is twofold: (1) Many generative tasks are interdependent (e.g., effective image editing requires robust grounding in text-image alignment), and (2) curriculum-based training, where foundational capabilities are learned before more complex ones, has been shown to improve generalization and sample efficiency (Bengio et al., 2009; recent ablation results summarized below). For example, in our experiments, models trained first on text-image grounding and then on editing outperform those trained in reverse order by 3\u20135% in FID and 4% in editing accuracy (see Table 2).\n\nThe capability-driven data infrastructure consists of three interoperable data engines, each responsible for a distinct supervision type, and a curriculum scheduler that sequences data presentation. The infrastructure operates as follows:\n\n1. **Text-Image Grounding Engine:** This engine constructs datasets by crawling public web sources and open repositories for image-caption pairs. We use CLIP ViT-L/14 (Contrastive Language-Image Pretraining, OpenAI, 2021) for relevance scoring, retaining pairs with a cosine similarity above 0.28 (empirically determined for a 95% precision rate on a validation set). Language model toxicity checks are performed using OpenAI GPT-3.5-turbo with a toxicity threshold of 0.2 (Perspective API scale). A random 5% sample is selected for human review, stratified by source and content type. Annotators (n=120; 55% female, 45% male; ages 21\u201354; self-identified as 40% Asian, 30% White, 15% Black, 10% Hispanic, 5% Other) are trained using a 100-example calibration set and required to achieve Fleiss\u2019 kappa >0.8 before contributing. Annotation guidelines, developed iteratively with annotator feedback and pilot studies, specify caption relevance, factuality, and sensitivity, with edge cases discussed in a shared manual. For example, ambiguous cases such as \"a person holding an object\" (where the object is unclear) are resolved by majority vote and, if needed, escalation to a senior annotator. Annotation error rates are tracked: in a 1,000-sample audit, the false positive rate for relevance was 2.1%, and inter-annotator disagreement occurred in 7% of cases, primarily due to subjective judgments on caption specificity. Disagreements beyond kappa thresholds are resolved by majority vote among three annotators, with escalation to a senior annotator if no consensus is reached. Caption experts are implemented as BLIP-2 models (Bootstrapped Language-Image Pretraining, Li et al., 2023) fine-tuned on 1M human-annotated captions; these standardize and enrich captions using a fixed prompt template. The engine outputs a corpus of 440M high-quality image-caption pairs.\n\n2. **Inter-Image Transformation Engine:** This engine assembles editing pairs by generating synthetic edits (using Adobe Photoshop batch scripts for color/style changes and the Segment Anything Model for object edits) and collecting real-world edits from the HIVE and MIT-Adobe FiveK datasets. Each pair includes the original image, the edited image, and a standardized transformation instruction generated by the same BLIP-2 caption experts. Consistency checks require a minimum 0.85 cosine similarity between the transformation instruction embedding and the actual edit description (using Sentence-BERT, a sentence embedding model). Annotators validate a stratified 5% sample, with inter-annotator agreement >0.78. The annotator pool matches the demographics above. Disagreements are handled as in the grounding engine. In a 500-sample audit, annotation error rate for edit correctness was 3.5%, with most disagreements arising from subtle color or style changes. The engine produces 120M editing pairs.\n\n3. **Image-Knowledge Association Engine:** This engine links images to structured knowledge entities by applying entity recognition with BLIP-2 and mapping to Wikidata using entity linking heuristics (minimum confidence 0.7). Manual validation is performed on a stratified 10% sample, with annotators trained on a 50-example gold standard and agreement measured (Fleiss\u2019 kappa >0.8). Annotator demographics and disagreement resolution follow the same protocol as above. In ambiguous cases, such as images with multiple plausible entities, annotators document rationale for their choices. In a 300-sample audit, the error rate for entity linking was 4.2%, with most errors due to visually ambiguous content. The engine outputs 27M image-entity pairs.\n\n**Data Integration and Interoperability:** Outputs from the three engines are merged via a shared annotation schema (JSON-LD). For example, an image-caption pair is linked to its knowledge entities and can serve as a base for generating editing pairs. Integration challenges include conflicting captions (e.g., two captions for the same image) and inconsistent entity mappings. These are resolved by majority voting among three independent caption experts and two human annotators, with ties broken by a senior annotator. In a 1,000-sample integration audit, 96% of merged records were consistent across all fields; the remaining 4% were flagged for manual review. Redundancies are detected by hashing CLIP image embeddings and deduplicating at the dataset level.\n\n**Curriculum Learning Strategy:** The curriculum scheduler organizes data presentation in three stages: (1) text-to-image (T2I) grounding, (2) editing, and (3) knowledge association. Stage transitions are triggered when validation loss (cross-entropy for T2I, L2 for editing, entity accuracy for knowledge) plateaus for 3 consecutive epochs (<0.1% change) and task-specific thresholds are met (e.g., FID < 10 for T2I, edit accuracy >85% before introducing knowledge data). These thresholds were selected based on ablation studies: as summarized in Table 2, models trained with T2I first, then editing, then knowledge, achieved the best performance (FID 8.2, edit accuracy 89%), outperforming all other orderings by 3\u20135% FID and 4% editing accuracy (p < 0.01). The rationale is that foundational grounding skills facilitate more complex transformations and associations, as initial T2I grounding enables the model to better interpret and apply subsequent editing and knowledge-based supervision. Empirically, reverse or mixed orders resulted in degraded performance, supporting this staged approach.\n\n**Evaluation Methodology:** We train multimodal diffusion models from scratch at 3B and 6B parameters using the curated datasets. Training is conducted on 512 NVIDIA A100 GPUs for 14 days (3B) and 28 days (6B), with mixed-precision optimization and fixed random seeds. Dataset splits are 90% train, 5% validation, 5% test, stratified by source, content, and demographic attributes. Baselines include Stable Diffusion v2.1 (open-source text-to-image model), Imagen Editor (official release), and a BLIP-2-based T2I model, each fine-tuned on our training split for 5 epochs with identical augmentation and optimizer settings.\n\n**Prompt Selection and Human Evaluation:** CPI-Bench, our new benchmark, comprises 50K prompts balanced across four domains: objects, scenes, edits, and knowledge associations. Prompts are sourced from public datasets (COCO, Visual Genome, HIVE, Wikidata) and manually curated to ensure diversity in content, complexity, and demographic representation. 10K prompts are held out for testing. Each domain is equally represented, and prompts are reviewed for ambiguity and bias by a diverse annotator pool. Human raters (n=50, minimum 2 years experience) are trained on a 100-example calibration set, provided with detailed instructions emphasizing realism, faithfulness, and editability, and required to achieve Fleiss\u2019 kappa >0.75 before participating. Each rater evaluates 200 randomly sampled generations per model. Inter-rater reliability is monitored throughout, with periodic recalibration. In addition, a user study with 100 non-expert raters (ages 18\u201360, balanced gender and ethnicity) was conducted on 1,000 generations, focusing on the same criteria. Statistical significance is assessed via paired t-tests (\u03b1=0.01, power >0.95), and effect sizes are reported for all comparisons.\n\n**Summary of Key Results (see Tables and Figures in Appendix):**\n- *Table 1*: Compares our infrastructure to prior pipelines, highlighting explicit capability modeling and curriculum scheduling.\n- *Table 2*: Summarizes ablation results for curriculum orderings, showing that"}}